      "commit_id": null,
      "commit_url": null,
      "created_at": "2026-10-30T14:57:43Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-30T14:57:43Z",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "body": "The `invalidateblock` RPC exercises a different code path. A simple way to reproduce is to force a development mainnet node to reorg a certain number of blocks at startup. For instance i was able to test this on top of aa76bd266df with the following clankerdiff:\n<details>\n\n<summary>Expand clankerdiff</summary>\n\n```patch\ndiff --git a/src/validation.cpp b/src/validation.cpp\nindex 9d1e1e3563..6cb3c2b0df 100644\n--- a/src/validation.cpp\n+++ b/src/validation.cpp\n@@ -3190,6 +3190,8 @@ void Chainstate::PruneBlockIndexCandidates() {\n     assert(!setBlockIndexCandidates.empty());\n }\n \n+static bool g_reorg_done{false};\n+\n /**\n  * Try to make some progress towards making index_most_work the active block.\n  * pblock is either nullptr or a pointer to a CBlock corresponding to index_most_work.\n@@ -3203,6 +3205,10 @@ bool Chainstate::ActivateBestChainStep(BlockValidationState& state, CBlockIndex&\n \n     const CBlockIndex* pindexOldTip = m_chain.Tip();\n     const CBlockIndex* pindexFork = m_chain.FindFork(index_most_work);\n+    if (const char* h{std::getenv(\"REORG_FROM\")}; h && !g_reorg_done) {\n+        g_reorg_done = true;\n+        pindexFork = Assert(m_blockman.LookupBlockIndex(*Assert(uint256::FromHex(h))))->pprev;\n+    }\n \n     // Disconnect active blocks which are no longer in the best chain.\n     bool fBlocksDisconnected = false;\n@@ -3381,7 +3387,7 @@ bool Chainstate::ActivateBestChain(BlockValidationState& state, std::shared_ptr<\n                 }\n \n                 // Whether we have anything to do at all.\n-                if (pindexMostWork == nullptr || pindexMostWork == m_chain.Tip()) {\n+                if (pindexMostWork == nullptr || (pindexMostWork == m_chain.Tip() && (g_reorg_done || !std::getenv(\"REORG_FROM\")))) {\n                     break;\n                 }\n \n```\n\n</details>\n\nI used it to disconnect 3449 mainnet blocks and observed memory usage increasing by about 14GiB until the fork point, and continuing during re-connection (i stopped it before reconnection was complete as my machine was about to OOM).\n\nAt a few MiB of additional memory usage per disconnected block, this is mostly a theoretical issue, as if an attacker can bring that to any meaningful level of memory usage, we have much bigger problems anyways.",
      "user": {
        "login": "darosior",
